<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muzaffar al-Din Gökböri</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1154">1154</a>–<a href="/years/1233">1233</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Gökböri, Kökböri</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muzaffar al-Din Gökböri was the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erbil">Erbil</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> from 1190 to 1233, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zayn-al-din-ali-kuchuk">Zayn al-Din Ali Kuchuk</a>. He married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabia-khatun">Rabi'a Khatun</a>, sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>, and fought at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Battle of Hattin</a> in 1187. He was famed for building hospitals, schools and hostels, and for his lavish public celebrations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mawlid">Mawlid</a>, the Prophet's birthday.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muzaffar-al-din-gokbori.jpg" alt="Muzaffar al-Din Gökböri" width="320" height="318" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Begtiginids._Muzaffar_al-Din_K%C3%B6kburi._AH_563-630_(AD_1167-1233)._)Arbil_mint._Dated_AH_587_(AD_1191-2),_obverse._Bareheaded_and_draped_bust_left;_name_and_titles_of_Muzaffar_al-Din_K%C3%B6kburi_in_outer_margin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gökböri, whose name means Blue-wolf in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-turkic">Old Turkic</a>, was born on 13 April 1154 into a Turcoman family, the Begtegīnids, associated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a>. When his father died in August 1168 the fourteen-year-old succeeded to the lordship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erbil">Erbil</a>, but the atabeg Kaimaz deposed him in favour of his younger brother. In exile he took service with the Zengid prince Sayf al-Din Ghazi II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>, who granted him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a>. He commanded the right wing of the Zengid army that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> defeated at the Horns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hama">Hama</a> on 13 April 1175.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Seeing Zengid power wane, he defected to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> in 1182 and invited him across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> into the Jazira, and his support helped reduce Zengid rule to little more than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>. Briefly arrested in 1185 on suspicion of collusion with Izz ad-Din Mas'ud of Mosul, he nursed Saladin through a grave illness at his castle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a> and was given Edessa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samsat">Samsat</a>.</p><p>At the Battle of Cresson in 1187 he led the army that destroyed a Christian force and killed Roger de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moulins">Moulins</a>, master of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a>; his Christian enemies knew him as Manafaradin. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Hattin</a> he commanded the Ayyubid left, and according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khallikan">Ibn Khallikan</a> he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>'s nephew Taqi ad-Din stood firm when the rest of the army was fleeing and led the counter-attack that decided the battle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>During the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> in 1190 his brother Zain ad-Din Yusuf died, and Gökböri gave up Edessa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samsat">Samsat</a> in return for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erbil">Erbil</a> and Shahrozur. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>'s death in 1193 he was in effect independent, acknowledging only the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>. Erbil became a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> learning: he patronised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khallikan">Ibn Khallikan</a> and the vizier Ibn al-Mustawfi, who wrote a history of the city in four volumes, and he founded the Dar al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">Hadith</a> al-Muzaffariya in 1198.</p><p>He kept his position through alliances, joining al-Muazzam of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> in an attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> in 1226. Without a male heir, he willed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erbil">Erbil</a> to the Abbasid Caliph al-Mustansir. He fell ill while campaigning against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> and died on 28 June 1233, and he was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gökböri” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son <b>Muzaffar al-Din Gökböri</b> married Rabi'a Khatun, sister of Saladin, fought at the Battle of Hattin in 1187 and received Erbil as an Ayyubid ally in 1190.</blockquote><a href="/state/begteginid-dynasty">Begteginid dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. Died in Balad, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.34,43.13&amp;year=1205" aria-label="Mosul on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Muzaffar al-Din Gökböri"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5897 4407h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5897 4407h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.34,43.13&amp;year=1205">What was here in 1205? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
